# V. Risk factors

#1 Macroeconomic cycle sensitivity
Credit activities are highly correlated with macroeconomics. During the recession, the decline in lending disbursements and credit inquiries directly affected corporate income.

#2 Regulatory and policy risks
- Regulators like CFPB have criticized the credit bureau for "oligarchy" and price hikes. Credit reporting costs have increased by about four times since 2022.
- If regulation changes the "tri-merge" policy or introduces more competition requirements, it could weaken the three branches of the institutional moat.
- The tightening of data privacy legislation may limit the scope of data collection and use

#3. Risk of technological subversion
- Open banking and alternative data sources may reduce reliance on traditional credit reporting in some contexts
- Advances in AI and big data technology may reduce the relative value of traditional credit data
- Continuous erosion of free or low-cost consumer credit products Consumer Interactive business

##4. High level of indebtedness
TransUnion has historically expanded through large acquisitions, accumulating a higher debt burden, and interest spending has significantly delayed net profits. In a high interest rate environment, rising refinancing costs are a risk that needs attention.

##5. Data security risks
The credit bureau has a large amount of sensitive personal information, and any data leak can result in substantial fines, litigation and reputational loss.

